{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 St. Louis Cardinals opened their season with a familiar mix of hope and heartbreak, a pattern that would define their entire year. On Opening Day, the bullpen immediately surrendered a costly single, but rookie JJ Wetherholt answered with a walk-off type swing that gave Cardinals fans reason to believe. Those early weeks in late March and April established the team's identity: scrappy enough to claw back into games, but not quite talented enough to win them consistently.\n\nThe late spring and early summer months offered genuine optimism. Jordan Walker emerged as the offensive engine the Cardinals desperately needed, collecting doubles and big hits with regularity, while Nolan Gorman provided power and Lars Nootbaar delivered a clutch home run in June that briefly ignited the fanbase. Iván Herrera quietly became one of the most reliable bats in the lineup, and for a stretch it felt like St. Louis might piece together enough to stay in wild card contention.\n\nJuly shattered those illusions. The bullpen — particularly Ryne Stanek, JoJo Romero, Luis Gastelum, and George Soriano — became a revolving door of disaster. Opposing hitters feasted on Cardinals relievers with home runs and extra-base hits that repeatedly swung close games away from St. Louis. A costly error by Masyn Winn on July 12th was emblematic of a team unraveling at the seams, and by late July the Cardinals were hemorrhaging losses at an alarming rate.\n\nAugust and September brought only intermittent relief. Riley O'Brien's repeated meltdowns — including a devastating grand slam allowed on August 20th and a crushing Randal Grichuk home run on September 12th that carried a -0.725 WPA — summarized the Cardinals' cruel fate: a lineup that could generate electricity, and a pitching staff that could not protect the lead. Late-season flashes from Iván Herrera, Alec Burleson, and rookie Leo Bernal gave fans something to cheer, but at 77-85, St. Louis finished well short of the playoffs, left to wonder what might have been if the bullpen had simply held on.",
